# Decode silicon for DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash

**A modeled hardware-architecture study of [deepseek-ultrafast](https://github.com/basetenlabs/deepseek-ultrafast):
where the time goes, what a custom accelerator would buy, and how much of that software can buy first.**
All MDA-1 and projected-GPU numbers are **simulated/projected**. Numbers marked *measured* are quoted from the deepseek-ultrafast README.

> **Status:** performance-model study plus one verified RTL block. No silicon, no synthesis, no GPU profiling.
> The claims were revised after an adversarial review ([what changed](docs/05_limitations.md#revisions-after-adversarial-review)).

## Abstract

deepseek-ultrafast serves DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash, a 552B-parameter MoE with FP4 experts, one request at
a time: {{ K.meas4.decode_tok_s|n }} tok/s on 4 x GB300 and {{ K.meas8.decode_tok_s|n }} tok/s on 8 x B300 (*measured*).
We rebuilt the workload from the engine's Rust/CUDA source and the model's checkpoint headers. We
then built an op-level performance simulator and calibrated it to the two reported step times.

1. **About half of every decode step is fixed latency, not weight streaming.** The calibrated model
   attributes {{ K.fixed4_ms|n(2) }} ms of the 3.30 ms step ({{ K.fixed4_share|pct }}) to dependent-stage
   and collective latency ({{ K.fixed_lo|pct }}-{{ K.fixed_hi|pct }} across every alternative calibration).
   HBM averages {{ pct(W.decode_per_gpu_tp4.total/1e9/K.meas4.step_ms/8.0) }} of peak.
2. **The fix is an execution model, not FLOPs.** One persistent, statically ordered program removes
   per-stage barriers. It streams the next layers' dense weights while the current layer's experts
   are in flight, and uses an unpadded expert layout and speed-of-light all-reduces. On the **same 4 x GB300**
   this projects **{{ C.gpu_df_tp4.tok_s_user|n }} tok/s/user ({{ K.sw_vs_meas|x }} the measured engine)**. The
   biggest levers are removing per-stage barriers (+{{ ((K.abl_by.A2.tok_s_user/K.abl_by.A1.tok_s_user-1)*100)|n(0) }}%) and
   cross-layer weight prefetch (+{{ ((K.abl_by.A4.tok_s_user/K.abl_by.A3.tok_s_user-1)*100)|n(0) }}%).
3. **Custom silicon built around that execution model matches it. It doesn't beat it.** MDA-1 is a
   decode ASIC: 2 dies, 8 x HBM3E at GB300's 8 TB/s, 2 PF FP8, a hardware MoE router (RTL-verified)
   and in-fabric all-reduce. It projects **{{ C.asic.tok_s_user|n }} tok/s/user**. Drawing all its
   assumptions jointly, that is {{ K.MC.vs_gpu_measured.p5|x }}-{{ K.MC.vs_gpu_measured.p95|x }} the measured engine (5th-95th percentile), and
   {{ K.MC.vs_sw.p5|x(2) }}-{{ K.MC.vs_sw.p95|x(2) }} the same-bandwidth GPU software. **Its case is economic:**
   {{ K.usd_vs_sw|x }} lower $/M tokens and {{ K.tpj_vs_sw|x }} tokens/J against GPUs running the same software
   (GPUs owned at $45K, drawing 60% of TDP). Across $25-65K per GPU and 40-100% of TDP those ratios span
   {{ K.usd_vs_sw_lo|x }}-{{ K.usd_vs_sw_hi|x }} and {{ K.tpj_vs_sw_lo|x }}-{{ K.tpj_vs_sw_hi|x }}. Break-even is about {{ K.be_sw_300|n(-3) }} chips at $300M NRE.
4. **Bandwidth alone won't fix it.** Rubin (19.2 TB/s) with today's per-stage latency projects only
   +{{ (K.rubin_vs_gb300*100)|n(0) }}% over GB300. With the dataflow execution model it projects {{ C.rubin_df_tp4.tok_s_user|n }} tok/s/user ({{ K.rubin_df_vs_gb300|x }}).

